  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur method focuses on constructing a near-Poissonian function with medium-scale periodicity to minimize the maximum overlap with its shifted versions. The core idea is to leverage Poisson distribution's sparse occupancy property within a periodic framework, which naturally creates regions with high occupancy separated by vacant gaps. This structure inherently limits the overlap between similar regions in shifted versions of the function. We combine Fourier analysis with adaptive thresholding to balance periodicity and sparsity, and use a non-gradient optimization method with constraint projection to enforce [0,1] bounds and unit integral while improving the overlap metric. The algorithm involves:\n\n1. Representing the function as a sum of Gaussians truncated to [0,2]\n2. Using Poisson salt-and-pepper noise simulation as inspiration\n3. Projecting onto a periodic grid to maintain uniformity\n4. Iteratively refining the coefficients and thresholds to minimize the maximum pairwise overlap\n5. Balancing the size of dense regions with thresholds determined by the integral constraint\n\nThis differs from standard gradient-based optimization by directly assembling candidate functions through a physical analogy of Poisson processes, which avoids oscillations around boundaries that plague traditional local search. The method is structured to maintain both high and low values effectively, unlike binary thresholding, and allows greater control over the materialization function's shape.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent the function as:\n    h(x) = \u03a3_{k=1}^{K} a_k * exp(-((x - b_k)/\u03c3_k)^2) ) clipped and adapted to [0,1]\n\nBut discretizing with Gaussians is complex. We use alternative:\n\nDefine candidate h(x) with:\n    h(x) = p(x)   where p(x) is a periodic binary representation with Poisson intensity \u03bb\n\nBut strictly better:\n\nOur candidate parameterization:\n    Let \u03a9 = [0, 2] be divided into M equal segments\n    Assign an integer-valued random field Z on segments with mean rate density \u03bb\n    Then h(x) = I{Z >= t} (indicator function)\n\nThis satisfies 0<=h<=1 and integral = (t) * (\u03bb * dx) <= 1, which determines \u03bb as integral=1.\n\nThe optimal solution should have a periodic structure that avoids constructive overlaps at common shift distances. We model this using:\n    h(x) = (\u03b8(x)) / max(\u03b8(x), 1)\n    where \u03b8(x) is a smoothed version of a Fourier series with coefficients chosen to approximate an ideal sparse structure.\n\nWe minimize:\n    C5 = max_k dx * \u222b h(x) [1 - h(x+k)] dx\n\nIn practice, we approximate via:\n    C5 = max_k ( dx * max_i h(i) * (1 - h(i+k)) actually no\n\nBut the proper discrete integral is the full linear correlation with dx factor:\n\n    C5 = dx * max_k (np.correlate(h, (1-h), mode=\"full\")[k] for appropriate k mapping)\n\nAll built-in uses dx=2.0/len(h)\n\nConstraints:\n    sum(h) = n/2 exactly\n    0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n\nWe'll implement using:\n    Initial: Poisson-inspired binary sampling\n    Refine: Fourier projection while maintaining integral constraint\n    Optimize: Iterative shrinkage with multi-directional expansion\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Parameter settings and initialization:\n\n   Input: initial_h_values if available (n=560), else None\n\n   Set:\n      n_points = 560         # base resolution (if no initial, we use)\n      target = n_points/2.0   # required integral\n\n   If initial_h_values available:\n      Use as starting point, but ensure sum(h)=n/2 by projection\n   Else:\n      Start with h_bin = np.random.binomial(1, 0.5, n_points)\n      Project h_bin to satisfy integral exactly: h0 = project_constraint(h_bin, target)\n      Then smooth with a Savitzky-Golay filter to create continuous candidates\n\n2. Fourier projection and filtering:\n\n   a) Compute Fourier series coefficients of current h\n   b) Truncate high frequencies to remove noise\n   c) Reconstruct and renormalize to maintain integral constraint\n\n   Code sketch:\n      n = len(h)\n      coef = np.fft.rfft(h - np.mean(h))\n      # Keep first M coefficients\n      coef[2*M+1:] = 0\n      h_proj = np.fft.irfft(coef)\n      h_proj = project_constraint(h_proj, target)\n\n   M values: start from M=10, gradually reduce from 50 to 1\n\n3. Iterative optimization with projection (after Fourier):\n\n   a) For current h, compute overlaps for all useful shifts (k=0 to 140 for n=560)\n   b) Perform polynomial fitting to target overlap behavior\n   c) Seek an elementary modification to reduce the maximum overlap\n\n   We use a quadratic approximation with gradient descent, but with line search and projection.\n\n   Helper: \n      compute_overlaps(h) -> returns overlaps list for all k\n      update_step(h, lg) -> returns h_new = h + lg (with clipping and projection)\n\n   Iterative process:\n\n      mg = -gradient(compute_overlaps, h)  # minimal reduction in max overlap gradient\n      t = line_search(compute_overlaps, h, mg)\n      h_new = project_constraint(h + t*mg, target)\n\n      while improvement and budget allows\n          evaluate new h and accept worst-case improvement\n\n4. Multi-resolution transfer and structured perturbation:\n\n   a) If time remains, upsample to higher resolution (n=1120, then 1680)\n   b) Use relative shift perturbations: small phase shifts in segments\n   c) Try different Poisson parameters \u03bb (mean intensity) for candidate initialization\n\n   For resolution increase (from n0 to n1):\n\n      Positions: new_x = np.linspace(0, 2, n1)\n      old_x = np.linspace(0, 2, n0)\n      h1 = np.interp(new_x, old_x, h)\n\n      Then re-scale to satisfy target integral\n\n5. Constraint handling and fallback:\n\n   Project_constraint implementation:\n\n      def project_constraint(h, target):\n          n = len(h)\n          bounds = np.array([[0,1]]*n)\n          # Use active-set method to enforce sum(h)=target\n          # Scale by Lipschitz coefficient and bounds\n          while True:\n              s = sum(h)\n              if abs(s - target) < 1e-5: return h\n              # Find which components are out-of-bound\n              ind1 = np.where(h < 0)[0]\n              ind2 = np.where(h > 1)[0]\n              # Adjust bounded points first\n              for i in ind1:\n                  h[i] = 0.0\n              for i in ind2:\n                  h[i] = 1.0\n              # Adjust sum\n              diff = target - sum(h)\n              if diff>0:\n                  # Add available margin without exceeding bounds\n                  for i in range(n):\n                      if h[i] < 1.0:\n                          margin = min(diff, 1 - h[i])\n                          h[i] += margin\n                          diff -= margin\n                          if diff<1e-5: break\n                  if abs(diff)<1e-5: return h\n              else:\n                  # Reduce smaple sum by shrinking from boundaries\n                  # ... similar to diff <=0 cases\n          return np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n\n6. Objective evaluation:\n\n   def compute_max_overlap(h):\n        n = len(h)\n        dx = 2.0/n\n        full_corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n        # We only consider positive and negative shifts up to n//2\n        # But the 'full' mode goes from -(n-1) to (n-1) in terms of lags\n        # And we want the maximum absolute value\n        max_val = np.max(full_corr)\n        return dx * max_val  # This is the objective to minimize\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n\n1. Primary parameter sweep:\n   - Start resolution: 180 (faster optimization cycles first)\n   - Target integrals: n/2.0 exactly\n   - Fourier truncation cutoffs: from 20 to 7 for different resolutions\n   - Gradient step sizes: adaptive from 0.1 to 5%\n   - Projection tightness: multiple passes with increasing constraint strength\n\n2. Structured variations to be tested (not all at once):\n   a) Gaussian kernels of 3-8 points width applied to peaks\n   b) Multi-directional adjustment of cutpoints in piecewise constant functions\n   c) Periodicity modulation with fourier coefficients of 4-7 frequency\n   d) Poisson parameter \u03bb controls varying from 0.3 to 0.7\n\n3. Budget allocation:\n   - Initial setup/fallback: 50s\n   - Poisson candidate evaluation: 100s\n   - Fourier projection: 50s\n   - Gradient-based local search: 500s\n   - General framework: 450s\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n\nRuntime complexity dominated by O(n^2 log n) in naive convolution and gradient evaluation, but our operations are:\n   - Covariance matrix of over 400 points * 560 x 560 -> ~200 million operations\n   Total for n=560, mode='full' correlation performance: approximately 150ms per call in low-level C\n\nFor n=1680 (final resolution), we might expect ~4x slowdowns per operation, but with optimizations:\n\n   Time breakdown:\n     - Initial: 100s (evaluation/perversion)\n     - Fourier steps: 5 full transfers (700 points->560, 1120,...,1680)\n     - Gradient steps: ~1000 iterations with fallbacks\n\nFallback plan:\n   If projected constraint fails (returns invalid h), then:\n      h = np.random.rand(n)*2.0-1.  # in [-1,1] then map to [0,2]\n      h = (h + 1)/2  # map to [0,1] initially\n      project(h, target)  # adjust to satisfy integral\n      return h\n\nValidation and return contract:\n\nAfter each evaluation:\n   - Check sum(h)*dx \u2248 1 (within 1e-6)\n   - Ensure all values in [0,1]\n   - Compare C5 value with current best\n\nUpon completion or time expiration:\n   - Return best_h, best_c5, processing_resolution\n   - Ensure final candidate has even length 560 points (Erds format)",
